  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Diurnal shower and thunderstorm chances remain a\nthreat today. Heat and humidity slowly trending down during\nSunday. Dry weather expected Sunday.\n\nTypical convective environment across southern New England this\nafternoon. One of limited bulk shear and modest CAPE values.\nIsolated thunderstorms already developed across portions of\nnorthern MA, with more across eastern NY state. The greatest\nrisk for stronger thunderstorms will be across western MA and\nCT. Still thinking this risk will diminish after sunset, \nalthough thunderstorms could still persist into the later \nevening. The greatest threat would be straight-line wind damage.\n\nDrier weather anticipated for Sunday. Favored the lowest dew\npoint guidance due to expectation of a W wind across the\ninterior. That said, still thinking it will take into the\nafternoon before dew points reach the lower 60s to upper 50s\nacross the interior. Towards the Cape and islands, dew points\nshould struggle to get much below 70 until some time Sunday \nnight. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Very warm and humid weather continues through \nWednesday. Daily shower and storm chances each day too, but \nlesser of a chance Sunday.\n\nOverall pretty persistent summer weather pattern really right \nthrough Wednesday, with enhanced quasi-zonal flow in mid levels \ninteracting with summerlike temperatures and humidity levels. \n\nWarm temperatures and elevated but not really oppressive \nhumidity levels (dewpoints in the mid to upper 60s) can be \nexpected to continue through Wednesday. General sameness to \n925-850 mb temps each day, only slightly cooling by a degree or \ntwo from Sunday through Wednesday, which supports highs in the \nmid/upper 80s and heat indices in the lower to middle 90s. While\nnot necessarily a hazardous combo of heat and humidity and most\nare already acclimatized to the summer warmth, thus not looking\nat headline- level heat, it is still an extended spell of heat \nindices in the low to mid 90s, so still continue to adequately \nhydrate and take breaks in cooler spaces.\n\nWith westerly mostly non-convergent low-level flow and modest \nheight rises Sunday, Sunday should end up being a generally dry \nday as will be tough to get enough lift to spark storms. \nOtherwise, daily shower and thunderstorm chances again resume \nfor early to mid next week, and with somewhat stronger flow, a \ncouple of those storms could become strong or severe in this \ntimeframe. The issue is timing the storms out, as has been the \ncase most of this week; this is very challenging as any ripple \nin the faster mid-level flow could focus better storm chances \ngiven the warm and humid weather. At this forecast time horizon \ntiming specific day(s) have more storm chances than others is a \ntough ask. Won't be raining and thundering all the time but \nthere will be storms to dodge here-and-there. \n\nTough to find many areas to improve upon NBM so stayed fairly \nclose to outputted NBM forecast values during this period.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Next week trending not as hot and not as humid \neach day, and possibly slightly cooler than normal by Fri into \nnext week.\n\nHudson Bay vortex slowly digs southeast through Ontario and\nQuebec next week, and in the process, advects numerous short \nwaves into SNE. Each short wave will slowly erode the northwest \nperiphery of the western Atlantic subtropical ridge over SNE. \nThis will result in a risk of scattered showers/thunderstorms \neach day through Thu. Although, not expecting a washout with \nmany hours of dry weather each day. Hence, a typical mid August \nweek for SNE. In addition, each short wave passage will result \nin temps and humidity coming down a notch each day. Thus, the \nweek will begin with warmer than normal temps along with dew pts\nin the 60s. Then finish the week with temps at or slightly \ncooler than normal by Fri, along with dew pts in the 50s!",
